The period from 1474 to 1538 spans the exploration and colonisation of the northwestern coast of [[Lurandia]] by the [[Zong|Kingdom of Zong]], from the first expedition to enter the [[Gulf of Louyang]] to the founding of [[Tamsui]]. The territory was constituted as the [[Sinchew Colony]] in 1544, following the [[Tamsui Plot]] and the division of [[Colonial Zong]] by royal edict of 1 June 1544.

At the start of the period the Zong presence in the New World consisted of [[Xinhai]], founded in 1456 on the southern coast, and the settlement at [[Anping]], founded in 1474, from which both were administered. The northern coast, including the whole of the territory that became Sinchew, had not been surveyed.

== First Gulf expedition (1474) ==

=== Purpose ===

The expedition was authorised by the colonial government at [[Anping]] and funded by the state. Its object was to establish whether a navigable route to [[Illypnia]] could be found by following the northern coast of Lurandia.

Zong had reached western Lurandia by sailing eastward across the [[Shendan Ocean]]. [[Illypnia]] lay beyond the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] on the far side of the continent, and no sea route between the two oceans was known. A passage around the northern coast would have connected them.

The expedition was commanded by [[Lîm Tiong-oán]] (林中遠), a naval officer of the Anping station, with [[Tân Sîn-hái]] (陳新海) as navigator and pilot and [[Ng Bûn-tiat]] (黃文哲) as cartographer. It sailed under the authority of Governor-General [[Ông Khéng-sêng]] (王慶生).

=== Voyage ===

The expedition departed [[Anping]] on 3 April 1474 and returned on 19 October of the same year, proceeding north along the coast and charting the shoreline as it went.

The coastal passage from Anping to the waters off the Hahkina measured approximately 7,441 km. The expedition continued a further 653 km westward before turning back, giving a distance of approximately 8,094 km in one direction and some 16,189 km for the round voyage.

=== First contact with the Hahkina ===

Off the coast of the plain south of the Gulf, the expedition encountered the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]]. The encounter was peaceful. Hachina accounts record that they came out to the ships and gave offerings, and that communication was minimal.

The Hachina did not take the Zong for gods. They had observed that the ships carried captured people of the region, whose speech resembled their own, and concluded that the strangers had conquered people of that kind and had come from the sea. The Hachina classified them accordingly, as equals but of the sea, against their own strength, which was of the land. They gave the Zong the name of the western empire, while holding themselves the central empire.

Zong records describe the Hahkina coast as well populated and the fisheries along it as substantial. This was the first recorded sighting of the Hahkina by the Zong.

=== Kawesa waters and the turn ===

Beyond the Gulf the expedition entered waters controlled by the [[Kawesa]] chiefdoms, whose territory lay to the north. The record is brief: the expedition reported that it was attacked by tribesmen while sailing near the coast, and that it fought its way clear. Neither the number of attackers nor the losses on either side were recorded.

In [[Chidoha]] territory further on the reception was more peaceful. By this point the expedition was running low on supplies.

Charting the coast as it went, the expedition established that the shoreline carried it steadily westward rather than eastward. The trend was inconsistent with the search for a passage to the Aethiopean, which required a north-easterly course. The expedition concluded that a large peninsula would have to be rounded before the coast turned east again, and that a voyage to its far side would require more time and supply than it possessed. The landmass was later charted as the [[Sinchew Peninsula]].

The expedition turned back. It returned to the Hahkina coast to refuel, this being the last shore at which it had met a friendly reception, and then made the passage south to Anping.

=== Outcome ===

The expedition did not find the passage. It mapped the coastline as far as the northern bend, established that the Hahkina were a substantial and organised people with whom trade was possible, and reported the Kawesa chiefdoms as hostile. Its report was the basis for a second expedition with greater resources.

== Second expedition (1476) ==

A second expedition was authorised at Anping in 1476 with enlarged resources and a longer intended range. Its object was to continue the survey beyond the point at which the first had turned back, and to establish whether the coast east of the peninsula would carry a vessel to the Aethiopean. It departed on 12 March 1476 and returned on 8 December, a voyage of some nine months.

The expedition proceeded further north than its predecessor. It entered and charted a large sheltered bay on the northwestern coast, at which [[Tamsui]] was later founded, and made contact with the chiefdom that held its shores. That chiefdom was the [[Akwesa]]. The meeting was the first between the Zong and the people from whom the site was later purchased.

The expedition turned back from the bay rather than continuing toward the passage, and returned to Anping by the route its predecessor had taken.

The two voyages and the surveys that followed them produced a chart of the northwestern coast. The peninsula was established as the northern limit of the known coast. The passage beyond it was not reached within the period.

No formal agreement with the Akwesa was concluded in the years following the expedition. Contact was maintained by private traders rather than by the colonial government until the 1530s.

== Private period (1476–1535) ==

=== Suspension of official voyages ===

The colonial government did not continue the programme of state expeditions after 1476. The administration at Anping directed its resources toward the consolidation of [[Louyang]], and for roughly the following fifty years no further state expedition was despatched to the northern coast.

Private voyages continued. The trade opened by the first two expeditions was taken up by merchants operating from Anping and the smaller outposts, and regular commerce developed with the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]]. Merchants extended the surveyed coast northward beyond the point at which the second expedition had turned back, and the results of these voyages accumulated in the records of the colonial administration without being formally commissioned.

Private traders, rather than the colonial administration, established the first sustained contacts with the [[Akwesa]]. No agreement with the chiefdom was concluded until the 1530s.

=== Temporary settlements ===

Several temporary settlements were established along the northern coast between 1476 and 1538. Most were occupied for periods of months at a time, as seasonal camps, trading stations or staging points for voyages continuing north. Few left any permanent trace.

The land of the Akwesa, at the bay where [[Tamsui]] was later founded, was among the most frequently used of these stopping points, its harbour being sheltered and its shore more receptive than the Kawesa coast to the north. It was used repeatedly across the period without a permanent settlement being established there.

The bay bore an Akwesa name, [[Oktalwa]], from ''oka'', "water", and ''talwa'', "town". The Zong rendered the name as 沃打 (Ok-tá), and the bay itself as 沃打灣 (Ok-tá-oân), the suffix 灣 meaning "bay". The place was known in colonial records by these forms for most of the period. It was replaced by the descriptive name [[Tamsui]] (淡水, "fresh water") when the settlement was founded in 1538.

=== Expedition of 1478 ===

The expedition of 1478 was led by the merchant [[Tân Khéng-hông]] (陳慶豐). It established the bay in the colonial record under a settled name, the Akwesa form [[Oktalwa]] in the Zong rendering 沃打灣, rather than as an unnamed anchorage. The name remained in use until the founding of the settlement.

=== Grant of 1511 and the Sūn-heng Hâng ===

In 1511 the Akwesa chief [[Hokta]] granted a plot of land at the bay to a party of Zong traders in exchange for ten pigs. The grant was recorded by the Zong as a purchase. Akwesa practice did not treat land as property that could be alienated; what was granted was the use of the site, and the ten pigs were the customary gift accompanying a grant of hospitality.

Akwesa accounts of the grant are not preserved. The Zong record of the transaction as a sale was not known to the Akwesa, who continued to regard the site as their own and its use as granted. No Akwesa objection to the Zong presence at the bay is recorded at any point in the period, and the difference in understanding did not become a matter of dispute between the two peoples.

A depot was built on the plot and was operated as a manned outpost from 1511 by the merchant house of [[Chiu Bûn-hái]] (周文海), trading as [[Sūn-heng Hâng]] (順興行). The outpost took the name Tamsui, and the plot granted for ten pigs was the whole of the territory the name originally described.

[[Chiu Bûn-hái]] was a merchant of Anping. Hokta's land lay between Kawesa chiefdoms that sought access to his bay, and the Sūn-heng Hâng provided a permanent armed presence at it in exchange for the site.

=== Alliance and the pseudo-state ===

The Sūn-heng Hâng assisted the Akwesa in their wars against neighbouring chiefdoms, and the territory under Akwesa control expanded. The company supplied men, shipping and money; the chiefdom provided land, legitimacy and access.

In 1521 the alliance was sealed by a marriage. [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]] (周慶玉), son of Chiu Bûn-hái and then a teenager, married [[Chinowa]], daughter of Hokta. The marriage was arranged by the two senior men. The Zong colonial record contains no account of it as a romance between the couple, and the later narrative of that kind is not supported by the contemporary sources.

The territory that resulted from the alliance was governed jointly by the two families and functioned as a polity. Following Hokta's death in 1527 it was ruled by Chiu Khéng-gio̍k and became known as [[Okheng]] (沃興), a Zong name formed from the first element of Oktalwa and the second element of Sūn-heng. Its relationship to the Zong colony was not defined, and it remitted no revenue to the colonial administration.

=== Settlement of Okheng ===

The territory was not settled by the company alone. From the 1520s the Sūn-heng Hâng conducted a campaign in Zong to attract settlers to Okheng, advertising the territory to landowners of the mainland as a place in which land might be bought, held and worked.

The company sold plots in the territory to landowners in Zong. It also undertook to transport the labour by which those plots were to be worked, carrying to the territory across the [[Shendan Ocean]] such labourers from the purchasers' own estates as the purchasers chose to send. The company acted as managing agent for the holdings it had sold, administering them on behalf of owners who remained in Zong and might never see the land they had bought.

The people transported were of mixed status. Estates in Zong held both serfs, bound to the land, and slaves, held as property, and the selection of whom to send was left to the landowners who made it. The company's part was carriage and management rather than ownership, and it did not enquire into the condition of those it carried.

The status of those transported was the same as it had been in Zong. The distinction between the serf, bound to the land, and the slave, held as property, was carried with them, and the territory did not create a condition of its own for the people who arrived in it.

The arrangement was possible because of the terms of the [[Zong Edict of 1500]]. That edict prohibited the enslavement of any person within the territory of Zong and criminalised the transport of enslaved persons into the kingdom. It did not address the movement of the unfree out of it, and it was never extended to the overseas territories. Labour leaving a mainland estate for the colony therefore fell outside the prohibition entirely — not by ambiguity in the edict's terms, but by its silence and by the limits of the jurisdiction in which it applied.

The consequence was immediate. From 1500 landowners in Zong who held slaves had reason to transfer them out of the kingdom before the prohibition took effect on their condition, and the colony was the destination to which they could be sent without restriction. The years that followed saw heavy traffic of enslaved people from Zong to Okheng, and the practice became one of the standing businesses of the settlement.

The traffic of this period is regarded as an early form of the [[Secret Trade]], the system by which enslaved peoples from [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]] were later carried to the Sinchew plantations.

The arrangement served both parties. Landowners in Zong acquired estates overseas without leaving their own, and their labour was moved to lands where it could be set to a crop the mainland did not produce. The company raised, by the sale of plots and by the charges it levied on the transport and management of them, the funds with which the territory was developed: the depot, the works and the campaigns against neighbouring chiefdoms were paid for out of the proceeds of the campaign of settlement.

The measure contributed to the growth of the Zong population at the bay, which was small in 1511 and grew steadily over the following decades as the campaign took effect. It could not offset the losses the Akwesa sustained in the epidemics of the 1520s, and the arrival of settlers did not alter the fact that the territory was overwhelmingly Akwesa in population throughout the period of Okheng.

