The Akwesa were the population of the territory throughout. The Zong presence, which began as a depot's company and garrison, grew through the settlement campaign to a small fraction of the whole, and the two communities lived at the bay together for the whole of the period.

=== Objection of the colonial government ===

Word of the arrangement reached [[Anping]] about 1522. The administration objected to the political character of the territory rather than to the marriage, which was a matter of private conduct. [[Chiu Bûn-hái]] was conducting diplomacy, waging war and expanding a territory in his own name, and dealing with a foreign chiefdom other than on behalf of [[Zong]].

The administration took no action. Its stated grounds were that the acknowledgment of the arrangement would concede the acquisition of a polity by a private merchant, and that its suppression would require an expedition to the far end of the coast. The correspondence was retained at Anping.

=== Akwesa Revolt (1528) ===

In 1528 a part of the Akwesa rose against the rule of the chiefdom, in the year following the epidemic that had killed Hokta. The revolt was directed against the leadership rather than against the Zong presence at the bay, and its stated ground was that the rulers had brought the plague upon the people by permitting the trading vessels to call at the settlement.

The revolt was suppressed. It is recorded in the colonial papers as the Akwesa revolt, and the papers give no account of its extent, its duration or the losses on either side. Most of the Akwesa did not join it, and the greater part of the chiefdom remained loyal to [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]], who had succeeded to the rule in the previous year.

The loyalty of the Akwesa to Chiu Khéng-gio̍k was both personal and institutional. He was an individual known to the people of the chiefdom, who had lived among them since childhood and was married into the family of the ruler he succeeded; and he was also the holder of the office of miko, the chieftainship by which the Akwesa were governed. The two grounds are not separable in the record. The allegiance of the greater part of the chiefdom survived the sale of 1535 and the division of its range, in which the same man was a party, and it survived his removal from office in 1544, after which he served the colony as its ambassador to the people over whom he had ruled.

The episode is significant for what it did not do. The revolt was not aimed at the removal of the company or of the Zong traders, and it did not become a general rising against the presence at the bay. It was a dispute within the chiefdom about who was answerable for the catastrophe that had befallen it.

=== Purchase of 1535 ===

The correspondence was acted upon in 1535, when the colonial administration acquired the site as the harbour for its northern presence.

The company was paid 4,000 taels of gold and was simultaneously threatened with invasion and with execution for treason should it refuse. The grounds for a charge existed in the unlicensed marriage, the conduct of diplomacy and war, and the dealing with a foreign chiefdom without authority. No charge was laid. The threat was not proceeded with, and the laying of a charge would have required the administration to establish in public that a merchant house had become a sovereign.

The sum was assessed as the value of the works the company had built, being the wharves, warehouses, depot and other improvements. No allowance was made for the land, which the administration held to be the colony's already by virtue of the grant of 1511, for the political entity, which it did not recognise, or for the trade the company had built, which it did not treat as property.

The transaction was recorded as a purchase.

=== Emancipation and land distribution (1535) ===

The conveyance of the territory to the colonial administration in 1535 made the enslaved population of Okheng a question of law for the colony. The administration answered it by freeing them.

The measures taken in that year voided the agreements under which the territory had been held and declared the Zong population of Okheng free. Those freed were permitted to claim land, and the administration established a system for its distribution. Land was granted to the freed men of the territory.

The distribution served two purposes. The first was administrative: the acquisition of the territory had left unresolved the question of who was to work the land that had been held by the company and the landowners, and who was to manage it, and the grant of land to those already upon it avoided the reassignment of holdings whose owners remained in Zong. The second was military. The freed men were expected to act as a militia for the defence of their own land.

The territory had been subject to incursions by [[Kawesa]] peoples throughout the period of Okheng and not merely in its last years. The raids were a standing condition of the frontier rather than a response to any particular measure, and they continued until the [[First Mahik War]] of 1538 to 1542 put the northern chiefdoms down. The militia raised from the freed men of 1535 was therefore directed against a threat that had been present since the settlement began.

The consequence for the Akwesa differed from the consequence for the settlers, and the difference was one of use. The Akwesa traversed the country and used it for grazing; the land was not held by them in severalty, and the grant of plots to freed men and settlers divided range over which they had moved and pasture on which they had run stock.

The settlers were granted the same land to put under a crop. Sugarcane was the plantation crop of the territory, and the plots distributed after 1535 were worked as cane land.

The climate of the bay suits the crop closely. The territory has a tropical monsoon climate, with a mean annual temperature of about 25.5 degrees and an annual rainfall of about 2,000 millimetres. The rain falls chiefly between September and May; the months of June, July and August are distinctly dry, with between 25 and 40 millimetres in an average month. Sugarcane requires abundant moisture to grow and a dry period to ripen, in which the withholding of water concentrates sugar in the stalk, and the bay supplies both: the crop grows through the wet season and is cut and milled in the dry months that follow.

Sugar is among the most labour-demanding of crops, which is the reason for the traffic in bound labour of the preceding decades. The plantation economy of Okheng existed because landowners in Zong held land in a place where cane could be grown and labour could be moved to it.

The grants therefore set one use of the land against another. Discontent among the Akwesa at the division of country they had grazed is recorded, but the greater part of the chiefdom remained loyal to Chiu Khéng-gio̍k, who continued to rule as miko after 1535 and who served the colony first as its provisional administrator and, after 1544, as its ambassador.

=== Transition ===

The family was not dispossessed. [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]] and [[Chinowa]] remained in the territory, and Chiu Khéng-gio̍k was appointed its governor.

The appointment was made on the ground that the retention of the sitting administrator would present the transfer of authority more easily to the Akwesa, to the settlers and to the employees of the company than his removal. Zong became the parent polity of the territory, which remained a sub-territory of the Zong colony, and Chiu Khéng-gio̍k continued as its provisional administrator.

== Tamsui Plan and the founding of Tamsui (1535–1538) ==

{{Main|Treaty of Anping}}

By the 1530s the colonial administration had determined that the Zong presence in the New World required a harbour on the northwestern coast, and selected the site at [[Tamsui]]. The plan drawn up in 1535 provided for the acquisition of the site from the merchant company that held it, and for an alliance with the Hahkina that would make its defence practicable. The company was bought out in the same year, and the agreement with the Akwesa was concluded in the course of the decade.

The administration's standing to dispose of the coast beyond the site was established by a claim of jurisdiction. [[Hokta]], who had died in 1527, was declared posthumously to have been King of the Northern Mahiks, the ruler of the whole region rather than of the Akwesa alone. The title is not attested in any document of his lifetime. On the strength of it the administration held that the northern chiefdoms had been subject to Hokta, that Hokta had granted the land to the Sūn-heng Hâng, and that the company had conveyed it to the colony in 1535 — with the consequence that the chiefdoms were subjects in rebellion rather than foreign powers.

The [[Treaty of Anping]] was concluded in 1538, and Tamsui was founded in the same year. Under the treaty the Zong recognised the Hahkina as a sovereign state and renounced territorial claims to the interior, in exchange for a fixed coastal presence and a formal system of trade and arms supply. The treaty also provided for the joint campaign that became the [[First Mahik War]].

